  "worked_solution": "Recall that $\\operatorname{cot}\u2061x=\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{tan}\u2061x}.$ Therefore, the given equation is equivalent to\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{tan}\u2061x=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}}=\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{3}.\n$$\n\nFirst, we find the principal value:\n\n$$\nx=\\operatorname{arctan}\u2061(\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{3})=\\frac{\\pi }{6}\n$$\n\nSince $\\frac{\\pi }{6}$ lies inside the given domain, our first solution is ${x}_{1}=\\frac{\\pi }{6}.$\n\nTo generate more solutions, we add or subtract integer multiples of $\\pi .$ Hence,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{2} & ={x}_{1}-\\pi =\\frac{\\pi }{6}-\\pi =-\\frac{5\\pi }{6}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the solutions are $x=-\\frac{5\\pi }{6},\\frac{\\pi }{6}.$\n\nFinally,\n\n$$\n{x}_{1}\\cdot {x}_{2}=\\frac{\\pi }{6}\\cdot (-\\frac{5\\pi }{6})=-\\frac{5{\\pi }^{2}}{36}.\n$$",
  "calculator_instructions": "",
